79237019574103,"slope":0.025010280111731273,"total":3.0},"snow_normal":20.7},"titles":{"year":"2026 so far: 10 daily record highs, no daily record lows","records":"Quincy has nearly stopped setting record lows","change":"Quincy's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986.","rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change"},"text":{"lede":"Yesterday's high of 42\u00b0F was typical for November 13.","t_yest":"November 13 was a typical day","p_yest":"The high of <strong>42\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13. The normal high for the date is 52\u00b0F, and the record is 77\u00b0F (1989, 1999). The low of <strong>28\u00b0F</strong> was typical for November 13.","t_rec":"Quincy has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Quincy's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Quincy has set <strong class=\"sig\">37 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 78 that chance alone would give, and <strong>73 record highs</strong>, close to the 78 that chance alone would give. The 1930s hold the most record highs of any decade (96). Quincy's all-time high is 114\u00b0F, on July 15, 1936.","t_chg":"Quincy's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days cooled over the long run but have warmed since 1986.","p_chg":"Across 121 complete years: Quincy's average low shows no clear change: \u22120.00\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.10 to +0.10) that includes zero. Quincy's average high has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.20\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.30 to \u22120.09), about \u22122.4\u00b0F since 1901. July and August highs have edged down across the full record; the 1930s summers were the hottest in it. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.44\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.04 to +0.85) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.61\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.11 to +1.11). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none. Highs rose in none; 5 months fell.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Quincy's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.04 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 36.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 1973 (66.60 in); the driest, 1953 (20.00 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 10 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}